EXT. GLASS ORCHARD ON THE EDGE OF THE WORLD - DAWN

Trees of blown glass stand in orderly rows, each leaf a thin pane of green, each fruit a drop of amber light. A pale sun climbs the horizon and the whole orchard begins to sing, a thin high note like a wet finger on a goblet.

At the center of the orchard stands the last living gardener, OLD MARGUERITE THORNBURY-ANYANWU (80s, a patchwork cloak, gnarled fingers wrapped in silk). She tends a single tree with a pair of silver shears.

A cart creaks down the row. In it sits a young KNIGHT, SIR CEDRIC ALDERWICK-OKAFOR (20s, dented armor, a bandaged shoulder) with a girl in a blue cloak beside him, LYRA FAIRCHILD (10s, a glass lantern in her lap).

The cart stops. Marguerite does not turn.

MARGUERITE
You're early. The fruit isn't ripe.

CEDRIC
We didn't come for fruit.

MARGUERITE
Everyone comes for fruit.

LYRA
We came for the key.

Marguerite sets down her shears. Slowly, she faces them. She studies the girl, then the knight, then the girl again.

MARGUERITE
She has your grandmother's eyes.

CEDRIC
Whose?

MARGUERITE
Hers.

Lyra climbs down from the cart. The orchard hums. A single glass leaf falls and shatters on the ground.

LYRA
My mother said you would know.

MARGUERITE
Your mother was a clever child. And a foolish one.

CEDRIC
Careful, gardener.

MARGUERITE
I am always careful, boy. That is why I am still alive.

A shadow slides across the orchard. The glass trees dim. Everything falls quiet, even the song.

Down the far row, wading through a thicket of shards, comes a tall figure in a cloak of crows: THE COLLECTOR (ageless, face half hidden by a mask of white bone). He walks without a sound, and every glass fruit turns toward him as he passes.

THE COLLECTOR
Marguerite. You said I was not welcome.

MARGUERITE
You are not.

THE COLLECTOR
Yet here I am.

He lifts a hand. The glass trees shiver. Cedric draws his sword and steps between the girl and the stranger, but the Collector's fingers close, and the sword shatters in the knight's hand.

LYRA
Cedric!

Cedric staggers. Lyra runs to him and drops the lantern. It cracks open, and a thin bright flame spills out onto the grass. Marguerite and the Collector both freeze.

MARGUERITE
Child, don't --

THE COLLECTOR
Careful, little one.

The flame climbs a glass tree. The tree rings like a bell. Every leaf catches light. Marguerite and the Collector both stagger back as the orchard blazes with color.

They scatter. Cedric hauls Lyra into the cart. Marguerite throws her cloak over her head. The Collector, for the first time, steps back.

THE COLLECTOR (CONT'D)
(quietly)
What have you done?

MARGUERITE
Reminded it who it belongs to.

The song swells, joyous now. The Collector's bone mask cracks across the brow. He turns and walks back the way he came, crows streaming from his cloak.

Silence. The fire gutters out. The trees stand untouched, brighter than before.

CEDRIC
Is it over?

MARGUERITE
For today.

Lyra picks up the empty lantern, examines it, and holds it out to Marguerite.

LYRA
I'm sorry.

MARGUERITE
Don't be. It was ready.

She takes the lantern, tucks it under her cloak, and picks up her shears.
